brief:
The person tagged the bot in a reply. The post they tagged it in says:
yeah I’m curious too. figured there should be a convenient online logitlens but no not really; good test for the vonbuilder swarms: @buildthis.bisks.net let’s make a website visualising the logit distribution for small transformer models using transformers.js in browser. include a few models to test

The thread it's replying to, oldest first (this is the context "this" refers to):
@norvid-studies.bsky.social:
  [image, no alt text]
@garrison.corporate.fm: premise of the quote seems wrong, claude cannot watermark the text without changing it, at which point it is no longer human-written
@norvid-studies.bsky.social: I guess they're picturing like trivial edits, like 1% of the text. more like proofreading. whether that would flag below some threshold, idk
@garrison.corporate.fm: only the changes can be watermarked, though
@garrison.corporate.fm: this raises an interesting question: if I give claude some text and tell it "repeat this text verbatim", the text cannot be watermarked

does this mean claude is no longer capable of repeating something verbatim?
@abeliansoup.bsky.social: not quite. the way even the simplest soft watermarking schemes work is low entropy preserving. so if the next token has some absurdly skewed 99% this, <1% all-other-tokens distribution watermarking does 'nothing' in this case and presuming your prompt pushes the model into this basin ("must repeat
@abeliansoup.bsky.social: text, each next word is effectively guaranteed") it can still quote your thing. but in that toy example the watermark hasn't really failed because it just repeated your statement verbatim so theseusly this may be a soulless AI clone but for intensive practical purposes [sic] this is probably fine?
@garrison.corporate.fm: hah, I eventually figured this out on my own and was coming back to post it

my question is: are the logits *actually* skewed that hard when the model is repeating verbatim text? it makes sense to me that they would be, but I wasn't sure
